        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 686,
            "height": 631,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "norm normalizes a list, array or tensor: it divides every element by the length of the whole, so the result has a length of exactly 1 and points the same way.\n\nThe list [3, 4] has length 5 (the square root of 3 x 3 + 4 x 4), so norm gives [0.6, 0.8]. [30, 40] gives the same [0.6, 0.8] - only the direction survives, the size is gone. Click the message in the example to see it.\n\nUse it to keep a direction and throw away a magnitude: which way a hand is moving regardless of how fast, a direction to point a light, a colour's balance regardless of its brightness.\n\nAn array of any shape is divided by its length taken over ALL of its elements. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random 4 x 4 array (left) is normalized (right): the pattern is the same, only scaled down so that the squares of all 16 cells add up to 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnorm\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnorm       a direction from a velocity\nnorm       balance a set of weights to a fixed overall size\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input divided by its length, the same shape as the input.\n\nSINGLE NUMBERS AND ZEROS:\nA single number has no direction, so norm of any single number gives 1.0. A list or array of all zeros has no length to divide by and gives nan (not a number) in every element.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nabs       the size of a single number\nsqrt      square root, for working out a length yourself",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 678,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 615,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "norm normalizes a list, array or tensor: it divides every element by the length of the whole, so the result has a length of exactly 1 and points the same way.\n\nThe list [3, 4] has length 5 (the square root of 3 x 3 + 4 x 4), so norm gives [0.6, 0.8]. [30, 40] gives the same [0.6, 0.8] - only the direction survives, the size is gone. Click the message in the example to see it.\n\nUse it to keep a direction and throw away a magnitude: which way a hand is moving regardless of how fast, a direction to point a light, a colour's balance regardless of its brightness.\n\nAn array of any shape is divided by its length taken over ALL of its elements. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random 4 x 4 array (left) is normalized (right): the pattern is the same, only scaled down so that the squares of all 16 cells add up to 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnorm\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnorm       a direction from a velocity\nnorm       balance a set of weights to a fixed overall size\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input divided by its length, the same shape as the input.\n\nSINGLE NUMBERS AND ZEROS:\nA single number has no direction, so norm of any single number gives 1.0. A list or array of all zeros has no length to divide by and gives nan (not a number) in every element.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nabs       the size of a single number\nsqrt      square root, for working out a length yourself"
        },
